// BT-3841 (ADR 0131): an evaluated literal block (`[...] value`) is an
// arm-like local-threading producer in class, value-type and actor methods.
// In the REPL it is one at an operand site of the input and in a REPL arm
// (BT-3823, `repl_producer_writes`); a statement or an assignment's whole
// value keeps the REPL's own lowering. The mutation analysis does pass a
// nested `[...] value` block's writes up to its enclosing block in every
// context, so this pins what a REPL `do:` body whose only write to a binding is
// inside a nested `[t := t + x] value` answers.

t := 0
// => 0

[t := t + 1] value
// => 1

t
// => 1

// A `do:` body whose only write is inside a nested `[...] value` threads the
// write back in the REPL (BT-3913 retired the Phase 0 error: a REPL loop body
// is a producer frame, and the nested `value` runs in place). The `do:`
// itself answers `nil`; `t` accumulates across the elements.
#(1, 2) do: [:x | [t := t + x] value]
// => nil

t
// => 4

// An arm holding one.
true ifTrue: [[t := t + 10] value]
// => 14

t
// => 14

// BT-3982: an evaluated block whose only write is inside a nested arm, at the
// REPL root and in an arm: the block answers the arm value and
// the binding is visible to the next input.
[true ifTrue: [x := 1]] value
// => 1

x
// => 1

true ifTrue: [[true ifTrue: [x := 2]] value]
// => 2

x
// => 2
